  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-4,0]?$",
  "worked_solution": "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-4,0],$ the function must be continuous on $[-4,0]$ and differentiable on $(-4,0).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $v(x)$ is continuous on $[-4,0]$ and differentiable on $(-4,0),$ so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $r(x)$ is continuous on $[-4,0]$ but has a corner point at $x=-1.$ Hence, $r(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=-1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-4,0).$ Therefore, $r(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $s(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2.$ Hence, $s(x)$ is not continuous on $[-4,0].$ Therefore, $s(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $t(x)$ is continuous on $[-4,0]$ but has a corner point at $x=-2.$ Hence, $t(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=-2,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-4,0).$ Therefore, $t(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $u(x)$  has a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ Hence, $u(x)$ is not continuous on $[-4,0].$ Therefore, $u(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.",
